<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <!--
    Bill of Materials for the AiPersimmon DDD building blocks.
    Consumers import this BOM (dependencyManagement with scope=import) to align the
    versions of every aipersimmon-ddd-* module without pinning each one by hand.
    Entries may be declared here ahead of the corresponding module being built:
    dependencyManagement is lazy, so a not-yet-built entry stays inert until a
    consumer actually depends on it.

    THIS FILE HAS NO PARENT, ON PURPOSE — and that is the whole reason it looks
    repetitive. An imported BOM contributes its EFFECTIVE model, parent inheritance
    included, so inheriting aipersimmon-ddd-parent published every pin the library
    happens to build against: importing this BOM alone brought 1600-odd managed
    coordinates with it (Spring Boot 3.5.10, Spring 6.2.15, Jackson, JUnit,
    Testcontainers, MyBatis-Plus, ShedLock). Worse than merely noisy: a BOM imported
    in a consumer's own dependencyManagement OUTRANKS what spring-boot-starter-parent
    supplies, so an application on any other Spring Boot line was silently pulled onto
    ours by depending on this library at all. A BOM must publish what its owner
    promises to keep aligned, and this library promises nothing about Spring Boot's
    version.

    Consequences of having no parent, all deliberate — and every one of them a thing
    that is normally inherited and here has to be written out. BomExportsOnlyItsOwnModulesTest
    fails the build when any of them drifts from the parent it was copied from:
      * The three third-party versions below are literals, since there is nothing to
        inherit them from. They are duplicated from aipersimmon-ddd-parent's
        properties, and BomExportsOnlyItsOwnModulesTest fails the build if the two
        drift apart.
      * This module's own version is declared here rather than inherited, and the
        aipersimmon entries still say ${project.version} — so the module list stays
        free of repeated version literals. Because the version is declared and not
        inherited, `versions:set` does not reach this module by default: the release
        must pass -DprocessAllModules=true, which scripts/release.sh does. Without it
        the BOM keeps the previous version while its ${project.version} entries then
        resolve to it, publishing a BOM that points at modules that do not exist.
      * distributionManagement is duplicated from the parent for the same reason. It
        does not travel to consumers — an import contributes dependencyManagement, not
        the deploy target — so this is publishing plumbing only. Without it `mvn deploy`
        fails on this module alone, before any other module is deployed, with
        "repository element was not specified in the POM".
  -->
  <groupId>com.aipersimmon.ddd</groupId>
  <artifactId>aipersimmon-ddd-bom</artifactId>
  <version>0.2.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  <packaging>pom</packaging>
  <name>aipersimmon-ddd-bom</name>
  <description>BOM aligning versions of the AiPersimmon DDD building-block modules.</description>

  <dependencyManagement>
